 # ADR-0003: Source pins for spec section 19 and G02 candidate dependencies
 
-Status: recorded (M00-T02, revised by M00-T02-fix and M00-T02-fix2). Task status: verified (recheck2, 2026-10-09). The v1 pins had an independent review (`tests/evidence/artifacts/m00-wf3/review-sources.json`: two major and several minor defects). M00-T02-fix addressed those, and an independent recheck (`tests/evidence/artifacts/review-sources/recheck/RECHECK.md`, 2026-10-09) confirmed the fixes. The recheck found one major gap (M1, the support matrix) and nine minor ones (m1-m9). M00-T02-fix2 addresses them (finding-to-fix map: `tests/evidence/artifacts/review-sources/fix2/README.md`). A second independent recheck (`tests/evidence/artifacts/review-sources/recheck2/RECHECK2.md`, 2026-10-09) found every fix correct, with no blocker or major. Its text-only items n1, n11, n12 and n15 are applied; its other minors (n2-n10, n13) are open and listed in progress.md.
-Date: v1 retrieval 2026-10-02T23:56Z; v2 baseline retrieval 2026-10-03T21:18Z; fix2 re-run and auxiliary pins 2026-10-09 (spec says sources were accessed 2026-10-01).
+Status: recorded (M00-T02, revised by M00-T02-fix, M00-T02-fix2 and M00-T02-fix3). Task status: verified (recheck2, 2026-10-09); the fix3 changes to the fetch tool and its docs are `implemented_unverified` until an independent recheck. The v1 pins had an independent review (`tests/evidence/artifacts/m00-wf3/review-sources.json`: two major and several minor defects). M00-T02-fix addressed those, and an independent recheck (`tests/evidence/artifacts/review-sources/recheck/RECHECK.md`, 2026-10-09) confirmed the fixes. The recheck found one major gap (M1, the support matrix) and nine minor ones (m1-m9). M00-T02-fix2 addresses them (finding-to-fix map: `tests/evidence/artifacts/review-sources/fix2/README.md`). A second independent recheck (`tests/evidence/artifacts/review-sources/recheck2/RECHECK2.md`, 2026-10-09) found every fix correct, with no blocker or major. Its text-only items n1, n11, n12 and n15 are applied. M00-T02-fix3 (2026-10-31) addresses its other minors n2-n10 and n13 (finding-to-fix map and the runs: `tests/evidence/artifacts/review-sources/fix3/README.md`); n14 is an integration item and is not addressed there.
+Date: v1 retrieval 2026-10-02T23:56Z; v2 baseline retrieval 2026-10-03T21:18Z; fix2 re-run and auxiliary pins 2026-10-09; fix3 tool and documentation changes 2026-10-31 (spec says sources were accessed 2026-10-01). No pinned hash value changed in fix3.
 
 ## Context
 spec.md section 19 cites 34 mutable primary sources (S01-S34) and says dependency versions and immutable snapshots must be pinned before implementation (plan 1.4, G01, R-QU-01, R-QU-07, R-DI-08). Later decisions (G02 and others) must cite fixed snapshots rather than moving docs. This ADR also pins the G02 candidate dependencies and adjacent tools, and records where the M00-T02 baseline/support matrix lives (section "Support matrix").
 
 ## Method (v2, reproducible; revised by fix2)
-- The fetch and normalization code is committed: `docs/sources/tools/fetch_pins.py` (python3 standard library, HTTP GET only, no credentials). Its docstring defines the normalizer (version 1: UTF-8 decode, drop head/script/style/noscript/template/svg content, NFC, collapse whitespace), the `compare` classes and every exit code.
+- The fetch and normalization code is committed: `docs/sources/tools/fetch_pins.py` (python3 standard library, HTTP GET only, no credentials). Its docstring defines the normalizer (version 1: UTF-8 decode, drop head/script/style/noscript/template/svg content, NFC, collapse whitespace), the `compare` classes and every exit code. Every GET has an overall per-source deadline (120 s; exceeding it is a failed fetch) and output is line-buffered (fix3, n8). Since fix3 a head without any `</head>` ends at the first start tag that cannot be in a head, where it used to swallow the whole document (n4); output is unchanged for every other document, so the normalizer stays version 1. `fetch` warns when a 200 response normalizes to under 200 characters.
 - For each source the script records HTTP status, raw-body sha256, normalized-text sha256, sizes, selected headers and date markers. The S01-S34 baseline is `docs/sources/fetch-baseline.json` (retrieved 2026-10-03T21:18Z). GitHub sources are fetched at the pinned commit (`raw.githubusercontent.com/<repo>/<sha>/<path>`) and the git blob sha1 is recomputed; all 7 match the pinned blobs.
 - Primary pin (`pin_v2.primary`). Since fix2, 14 sources use the raw-body sha256: the 7 GitHub permalinks (S16-S19, S27, S29, S34; immutable by commit) and the 7 OpenBSD/OpenSSH pages (S01-S05, S10, S30), whose raw hash held from the v1 retrieval (2026-10-02T23:56Z) to the fix2 re-run (2026-10-09T05:31Z). The other 20 use the normalized-text sha256: CMS or static-site HTML (S06-S09, S11-S15, S20-S23, S28) and the `.md` variants of doc sites (S24-S26, S31-S33), whose bytes can change in a site rebuild without a text change. The raw hash is kept for all as evidence of the exact bytes fetched. fix2 moved S23 from raw to normalized (section "Pin changes in M00-T02-fix2").
 - Reproducibility proof (executed, 2026-10-03): `fetch` run twice back to back gave 34/34 identical raw hashes and 34/34 identical normalized hashes and date markers (`docs/sources/fetch-repeat.json` vs `fetch-baseline.json`, `compare` exit 0), and the 27 snapshot files were byte-identical across the two runs. That shows stability over minutes. Over longer intervals: 21 sources kept their v1 raw bytes for about 21 h (v1 to v2; 13 did not), and the fix2 re-run after about 5.3 days found raw bytes unchanged for 19 and normalized text unchanged for 32 of 34 (`pins.json` `reproducibility`).
 - v1 hashes. For the 28 HTML and GitHub sources, the v1 `text_sha256` came from an HTML-to-text script that was not retained, and the independent review could not reproduce it. It remains in `pins.json` as `pin.legacy_text_sha256_unreproducible` and must not be used as a drift detector. For the 6 `.md` sources (S24-S26, S31-S33), the v1 `text_sha256` equals the v2 raw-body sha256: v1 hashed the same raw `.md` bytes 21 h earlier. fix2 relabelled these 6 as `pin.legacy_raw_body_sha256_v1` (reproduced), following the recheck's `legacy_v1_hashes.out`. The `pin.value` fields that still held v1 text hashes (13 sources) now say "superseded". The v1 sentence "all 34 text hashes matched on re-fetch" is withdrawn as unverifiable.
-- `compare` (revised by fix2) gives each source one class, with the HTTP status taken into account: IDENTICAL, RAW_ONLY (text and markers equal, bytes differ), MARKER_ONLY (text equal, a date marker moved), CONTENT (visible text changed, or a GitHub body whose blob sha1 is not the pinned blob), FETCH_FAILED (either side not HTTP 200, for example a bot-challenge 403; hashes not compared), MISSING, EXTRA. It also reports `primary_same`: whether the new file still matches the `pin_v2` primary hash in `pins.json`, which it must be able to read unless `--no-pins` is given. Exit 0 = all identical, 4 = text identical with only bytes or markers moved, 3 = fetch failure, 2 = content change or missing source; 1 = unreadable input, 64 = usage error. The v1 compare counted a moved date marker as a content change and ignored HTTP status. All requests are GET over https only, also after redirects.
-- GitHub branch drift. `fetch` reads the pinned commit, so `compare` cannot see later changes on the branch the spec URL names. `fetch_pins.py upstream` GETs the raw file on that branch (the raw rendering of the spec URL; HEAD for repo-root URLs), compares its blob sha1 with the pin, and counts the quoted fragments still present. Exit 0 = no drift, 4 = drift with every quoted fragment still present, 2 = a drifted source lost a quoted fragment, 3 = fetch failure.
+- `compare` (revised by fix2 and fix3) gives each source one class, with the HTTP status taken into account: IDENTICAL, RAW_ONLY (text and markers equal, bytes differ), MARKER_ONLY (text equal, a date marker moved), CONTENT (visible text changed, or a GitHub body whose blob sha1 is not the pinned blob), GONE (reference HTTP 200, second file HTTP 404 or 410: the page was removed or moved; fix3, n3), FETCH_FAILED (either side not HTTP 200 otherwise, a network error or the per-source deadline; hashes not compared), MISSING, EXTRA. It also reports `primary_same`: whether the new file still matches the `pin_v2` primary hash in `pins.json`, which it must be able to read unless `--no-pins` is given. Off-pin rule (fix3, n2; the same rule is in `docs/sources/README.md` and `pins.json` `reopen_conditions`): `primary_same=no` is the off-pin flag. `compare` always prints such a line (also without `--all`), counts it as `off_pin`, and exits 5 when nothing worse was found. Against the baseline, an off-pin RAW_ONLY or MARKER_ONLY line means the text equals the pin and only the raw bytes of a raw-primary source moved: pin maintenance, not drift. An off-pin normalized-primary source is CONTENT. Exit codes, first match wins: 2 = CONTENT, GONE or MISSING; 3 = FETCH_FAILED; 5 = off-pin and nothing above; 4 = only RAW_ONLY / MARKER_ONLY, every one on-pin; 0 = all identical and none off-pin; 1 = unreadable input, 64 = usage error. `fetch` exits 0, 2 (a GitHub body whose blob sha1 is not the pinned blob, the integrity failure `compare` calls CONTENT, also exit 2) or 3 (a source not HTTP 200, a network error or the deadline); before fix3 all three were exit 1, which is now only an input error (n9). The v1 compare counted a moved date marker as a content change and ignored HTTP status. All requests are GET over https only, also after redirects.
+- GitHub branch drift. `fetch` reads the pinned commit, so `compare` cannot see later changes on the branch the spec URL names. `fetch_pins.py upstream` GETs the raw file on that branch (the raw rendering of the spec URL; HEAD for repo-root URLs), compares its blob sha1 with the pin, and counts the quoted fragments still present. The URL it builds is recorded for all 7 GitHub sources in `pins.json` (`upstream.url` and `upstream.ref`; S19, S27 and S29 use HEAD with the pinned path README.md, S16-S18 and S34 the branch named in the spec URL), and `upstream` and `selftest` check the record against the code (fix3, n13). Exit 0 = no drift, 4 = drifted, quotes intact (claim not re-read), 2 = a drifted source lost a quoted fragment or has none to check, or its file is gone (404 or 410), 3 = fetch failure. The old label "benign drift" is withdrawn: a surviving quote shows that the quoted words are present, not that the claim still holds (n5).
 - Release tags. `pins.json` `tag_pins` records the commit each candidate release tag pointed to on 2026-10-09, read from the repository's public git ref advertisement (GET `https://github.com/<repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-upload-pack`, protocol v0, no POST). `fetch_pins.py tags` re-checks them. Tag names stay as labels.
-- Excerpt attribution. `fetch_pins.py excerpts` checks that every quoted fragment of every `pins.json` excerpt occurs in its own source's pinned copy, and names any other source that contains a missing fragment (the S26 misattribution class). It exits 3 if any source could not be checked (a GitHub source without `--fetch-git`, or an excerpt with no quote). S05 and S21 had no quoted fragment, so fix2 added verbatim quotes from their snapshots. On 2026-10-09 every one of the 38 sources was checked and every fragment found (counts in the fix2 README).
+- Excerpt attribution. `fetch_pins.py excerpts` checks that every quoted fragment of every `pins.json` excerpt occurs in its own source's pinned copy, and names any other source that contains a missing fragment (the S26 misattribution class). Matching is on whole word sequences since fix3 (n10): a fragment must start and end on word boundaries, so "is not permitted by default" no longer matches "is not permitted by defaults". It exits 3 if any source could not be checked (a GitHub source without `--fetch-git`, or an excerpt with no quote). S05 and S21 had no quoted fragment, so fix2 added verbatim quotes from their snapshots. On 2026-10-09 every one of the 38 sources was checked and every fragment found (counts in the fix2 README).
 - Immutable copy. GitHub sources: commit permalink plus blob sha1. The other 27 spec sources and A01-A04: a deterministic gzip of the normalized text in `docs/sources/snapshots/` (scope, verification and copyright note in its README; owner decision E8). The Internet Archive's read-only availability API (GET only, no save requests) returned a snapshot for 8 of 27 sources across two passes, and its answers were inconsistent between passes. Every snapshot it found predates the pin, and only S03's archived text matches the pinned normalized text (`docs/sources/archive-lookups.json`). Archive URLs are recorded as hints, not as the copy of record.
 - Date markers kept from v1 where they exist: OpenBSD man page footer date, openssh.org Last-Modified, Microsoft ms.date and updated_at, Smallstep "Last updated" text, 1Password embedded dateModified. They are now extracted by the script.
 - Candidate versions came from the crates.io API, the npm registry, the Go module proxy and `gh api` GET, and tag commits from git ref advertisements (fix2). No installs, no writes, no SSH or credential access, no fixture downloads.
@@ -118,6 +118,17 @@ The "construct the baseline/support matrix" half of M00-T02 (plan.md M00-T02 row
 - The 13 `pin.value` fields that held v1 text hashes (S14, S15, S20-S26, S28, S31-S33) now say "superseded". Their v1 hashes stay under `legacy_*` names (finding m4).
 - New pins: A01-A04 with snapshots (finding m5), and `tag_pins` for 14 tags in 10 repositories (finding m8). No existing source pin or hash value was changed. `fetch-baseline.json` and `fetch-repeat.json` are unchanged.
 
+## Changes in M00-T02-fix3 (tool and documentation; no pinned hash changed)
+Finding-to-fix map (recheck2 section F; runs and exit codes: `tests/evidence/artifacts/review-sources/fix3/README.md`). Exit codes by subcommand are in `docs/sources/README.md` "Exit codes" and in the script docstring.
+- n2: off-pin lines are always printed, `off_pin` is counted in the header, and compare exits 5 when nothing worse was found. One rule in README, this ADR ("Reopen conditions") and `pins.json`.
+- n3: GONE class (404 or 410 after a 200 reference), exit 2; `upstream` reports a gone file as exit 2; persistent FETCH_FAILED rule.
+- n4: head without `</head>` handled (normalizer stays version 1); selftest cases; stderr warning below 200 characters.
+- n5: exit-4 wording and the re-read policy ("Reopen conditions"); a drifted source with no quoted fragment is exit 2 instead of 4.
+- n8: 120 s overall deadline per GET (`fetch --deadline`), mapped to FETCH_FAILED; line-buffered output.
+- n9: `fetch` exit 2 for a GitHub blob mismatch, 3 for a failed fetch, 1 only for input errors.
+- n10: whole-word excerpt matching. n13: `upstream` URL recorded for the 7 GitHub sources.
+- Beyond the findings: the S29 drift note above (a live `upstream` run lost a quoted fragment, so the new exit 2 fired on a real change).
+
 ## Contradictions and nuances found (escalations, not decided here)
 - E1 (S06/S07 vs spec ~line 323): suggested spec clarification, not a contradiction. The spec sentence (remembered approvals do not keep private keys available after lock) is supported as written. S07 also says the timed options (4/12/24 h) keep key approvals in agent memory while 1Password is locked, with an unlock still needed for key access. A sentence distinguishing approvals from key availability would prevent misreading; wording is the owner's.
 - E2 (S01/S02 vs spec ~line 488): "ssh -G ... Match exec may run commands" is an inference, labelled as such. S01 says "The exec keyword executes the specified command under the user's shell" and S02 says "-G ... after evaluating Host and Match blocks"; together they support it textually but no source says it in one sentence. Needs behavioral evidence (G07/G09).
@@ -140,7 +151,8 @@ The "construct the baseline/support matrix" half of M00-T02 (plan.md M00-T02 row
   - S18: main blob 4f3e062 (pinned 367d911).
   - S34: main blob 132b655 (pinned 2428783).
 
-  Every pin is a commit permalink; `fetch` reads that commit and the blob sha1 matches. All quoted excerpt fragments still occur on main (2/2, 2/2, 3/3, 3/3). The commits that changed S16, S18 and S34 were not looked up, because no GitHub API request was made. The S19, S27 and S29 READMEs on the default branch still equal their pinned blobs.
+  Every pin is a commit permalink; `fetch` reads that commit and the blob sha1 matches. All quoted excerpt fragments still occur on main (2/2, 2/2, 3/3, 3/3); that is exit 4, "drifted, quotes intact (claim not re-read)". The claims of these four sources were not re-read against main; the re-read policy under "Reopen conditions" applies when a still-open gate cites one of them. The commits that changed S16, S18 and S34 were not looked up, because no GitHub API request was made. The S19, S27 and S29 READMEs on the default branch still equal their pinned blobs.
+- Drift observed again by `upstream` at 2026-10-31T20:39Z (fix3 run, `tests/evidence/artifacts/review-sources/fix3/48_upstream.out`): S16, S17, S18 and S34 changed again on `main` (new branch blobs; all quoted fragments still present, exit-4 class; claims not re-read). S29 is new: its README on HEAD changed (blob 5141426 against the pinned 6f2523a) and 1 of 2 quoted fragments is gone ("Development requires Bun 1.4.1 or later and Zig 0.16.0."; HEAD says Zig 0.17.0), so `upstream` exits 2. The diff was read in fix3 and the S29 claim still reads as supported on HEAD (recorded in the S29 `note`); the pin at de8dc97 is unaffected. Whether a still-open gate relies on OpenTUI's Zig requirement is not checked here: that decision, under "Reopen conditions", is the gate owner's. S19 and S27 still equal their pinned blobs.
 - S26 excerpt: the v1 excerpt quoted "Be very specific about your end goal", which is on S31, not S26. Corrected in `pins.json` and `excerpts/notes.md`; the S26 claim is supported by its own text. On 2026-10-09 S26 had an unrelated content edit (Fast-mode / EU data residency); all 5 S26 excerpt fragments are still live.
 - S24 excerpt: the first quote joins three list headings that are not adjacent on the page; ellipses now mark the gaps (finding m9).
 - S27 pin: 96d69d2 is the main head at retrieval, not the v2.0.10 release commit d5bfd5c; cite the tag for the release.
@@ -152,8 +164,13 @@ The "construct the baseline/support matrix" half of M00-T02 (plan.md M00-T02 row
 - The revised fetcher's own runs (`fetch`, `compare`, `verify`, `upstream`, `tags`, `excerpts`, `selftest`) are listed in the fix2 README with their exit codes.
 
 ## Reopen conditions
-- `fetch_pins.py compare` against `docs/sources/fetch-baseline.json` (or `fetch-baseline-aux.json`) classifies a source cited by a still-open gate as CONTENT, or reports `primary_same=no` for it. Read `docs/sources/snapshots/<id>.txt.gz` to see what the pin said. MARKER_ONLY, and RAW_ONLY with `primary_same=yes`, are not drift. FETCH_FAILED means re-run.
-- `fetch_pins.py upstream` exits 2: a GitHub source drifted on its spec branch and lost a quoted fragment. `fetch_pins.py tags` exits 2: a tag moved or is missing.
+- `fetch_pins.py compare` against `docs/sources/fetch-baseline.json` (or `fetch-baseline-aux.json`) classifies a source cited by a still-open gate as CONTENT, GONE (HTTP 404 or 410, after one confirming re-run) or MISSING. Read `docs/sources/snapshots/<id>.txt.gz` to see what the pin said. This is the one off-pin rule (also in `docs/sources/README.md` and `pins.json` `reopen_conditions`; fix3, n2):
+  - `primary_same=no` flags a source whose second-file primary hash differs from the pin. `compare` always prints it and exits 5 when nothing worse was found.
+  - Against the baseline, off-pin with class RAW_ONLY or MARKER_ONLY means the text equals the pin and only the raw bytes of a raw-primary source moved. That is pin maintenance (run `verify`, then re-pin with a dated note, as for S23 in fix2), not drift and not a reopen. An off-pin normalized-primary source is CONTENT, which is drift.
+  - MARKER_ONLY, and RAW_ONLY with `primary_same=yes`, are not drift.
+  - FETCH_FAILED means re-run, not drift, unless the same source is FETCH_FAILED on runs at least 24 h apart: then treat it as GONE and investigate (n3).
+- `fetch_pins.py upstream` exits 2: a GitHub source drifted on its spec branch and lost a quoted fragment or has none to check, or its file is gone. `fetch_pins.py tags` exits 2: a tag moved or is missing.
+- Re-read policy for a drifted GitHub source (`upstream` exit 4, "drifted, quotes intact (claim not re-read)"; n5). Exit 4 reopens nothing by itself, and a surviving quote does not show that the claim still holds. For a source cited by a still-open gate: (1) read the branch file against the claim before anyone relies on `main`; (2) record the outcome in the source's `note` with the date, as `claim re-read: holds` or `claim re-read: changed`; (3) reopen the gate if the claim changed or the file is gone (exit 2); (4) until step 2 is recorded, cite only the pinned permalink, which stays valid. A drifted source that no open gate cites needs only the note.
 - A candidate ships a new minor/major release before the G02 selection is locked.
 - Win32-OpenSSH (`PowerShell/Win32-OpenSSH`) publishes a release tag without "Preview", or `PowerShell/openssh-portable` changes its `LICENCE`. Iroh JS adds darwin-x64, or A02 changes the Go binding's status or platforms. Pigeons changes its ProxyCommand integration, telemetry or service behavior.
 - spec section 19 is amended, or `docs/compatibility/matrix.md` cites a source not listed here or in the G03 source pins.
@@ -161,6 +178,7 @@ The "construct the baseline/support matrix" half of M00-T02 (plan.md M00-T02 row
 ## Limits
 - Normalized-text hashes depend on the normalizer: a change of Python's `html.parser` or `unicodedata` data could shift a hash without a content change. The baseline and the fix2 runs used Python 3.14.8. A mismatch should be checked against the snapshot before it is treated as drift.
 - Long-interval stability is shown for one interval per source: about 21 h (v1 to v2), and about 5.3 days to the fix2 re-run. The auxiliary sources A01-A04 have only a same-day pair of runs.
-- `upstream` reads the branch HEAD at one moment; it does not list the commits that changed a file. Release asset digests are not recorded (see Candidate dependency pins).
+- Reproduction depends on the interpreter's TLS stack: on 2026-10-31 Python 3.15.2 with OpenSSL 4.1.0 could not fetch S01-S05, S10 and S30 (`man.openbsd.org`, `www.openssh.org`; `TLSV1_ALERT_DECODE_ERROR` in a direct probe; the pre-fix3 tool failed the same way), while Python 3.13.18 with OpenSSL 3.5.9 could. The cause on the servers' side is not established.
+- The per-source deadline bounds one source, not a whole run, and a stalled run was seen once without an established cause (recheck2 n8); fix3 bounds it and flushes progress lines but does not explain it. `upstream` reads the branch HEAD at one moment; it does not list the commits that changed a file. Release asset digests are not recorded (see Candidate dependency pins).
 - The Internet Archive availability API gave inconsistent answers; absence of an archive URL is not proof of absence.
 - Behavioral claims (E2 Match exec under `ssh -G`, OpenTUI under Node 26.4 versus Bun, Iroh JS on Intel macOS) are not tested here; they belong to G02, G07 and G09.
